Spec: specs/002-capture-and-canonical-events/spec.md (Redaction; supports AC27, AC29)
Why this approach: the header side clones first and rewrites values in the clone, matching names with strings.EqualFold so non-canonical keys set directly are caught too; the query side splits by hand on & and the first =, so only a secret parameter's value changes and the rest of the raw query is returned unchanged (the original string itself when nothing matched).
Alternatives rejected: url.ParseQuery / url.Values.Encode (reorders, re-escapes, and drops ;-bearing or badly escaped parameters); redacting a header to one [REDACTED] value (loses how many values were sent); naming a provider header in the core test (purity_test forbids it, so the test uses X-Test-Key).
Trade-off / known limit: query names match case-sensitively after url.QueryUnescape (+ is a space); a name that does not unescape is compared as sent; a bare parameter with no = has no value and is kept. Recorded in plan.md.
Verified by: make verify; go test -race ./internal/core -run TestRedact (TestRedact_HeaderClone, TestRedact_HeaderNamesKept, TestRedact_QueryKeepsBytes); tests first failed to build on the missing redactHeader and redactQuery.
